  <div class="section-heading">Highlights of Gardens at the LSU AgCenter's Hammond Research Station</div>
  <p>By <span data-field="author_name">{{ author_name }}</span>, Professor (Horticulture), LSU AgCenter;</p>
  <p>Many residents of southeast Louisiana and southern Mississippi may not know that the LSU AgCenter has an agricultural experiment station in this area devoted to horticulture research and extension programs to aid commercial nursery and landscape professionals, along with homeowners. Efforts at the station in 2005 changed from a focus on vegetable and fruit crops to an emphasis on landscape horticulture.</p>

  <div class="two-column-layout">
    <div class="column">
      <p>Faculty at the Hammond Research Station work on weed control, storm water management, entomology, landscape plant evaluation, urban forestry and much more.</p>
      <p>An urban forest area was planted after Hurricane Katrina in 2005 and includes 32 species of shade trees. Over time, these trees will provide research opportunities in suitability for urban uses and maintenance practices. The use of truly native trees will also be studied and promoted, as will cultivar evaluations and cultivation requirements of lesser-known native plants.</p>
      <p>The Arboretum emphasizes at the station is the evaluation of annual bedding plants, herbaceous perennials, roses, new shrubs and other companion plants. In the "sun garden," small island groupings of ornamental shrubs, annuals and perennials showcase some of the newer introductions in the plant world. Beautiful yet, bus can they take the heat? The performance of these new plants is evaluated in full sun conditions until all the heat and humidity that a Louisiana summer can bring. We evaluate over 800 varieties of cool season and warm season plants each year.</p>
      <p>A "shade garden" area is also included now with over 200 varieties of shade requiring plant varieties being evaluated annually. Caladiums and hosta are a major research focus.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="image-container">
      <img src="placeholder_image_1.jpg" alt="Garden scene">
      <p class="caption">Sun Garden at Hammond Research Station. (Image by <span data-field="image_credit_1">{{ image_credit_1 }}</span>)</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="clear: both; margin-top: 20px;">The Maggie Jenkins Azalea Garden at the station was established in 2006 to provide a continuing feature to educate people about azaleas and native plants. It is named for Maggie Y. Jenkins, owner of Jenkins Farm and Nursery in Amite, and recognizes the enormous contribution of "Mrs. Maggie" in promoting azaleas and native plants to the green industry. The garden currently includes Robin Hill, Encore, Crimson and Southern Indian families of azaleas. More than 50 different species of native trees and shrubs can be found scattered among the azaleas. The garden is ever expanding and more azaleas will be added in the future.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="section-heading">Director's Notebook:</div>
  <p>In April, MSU Ag Communications announced that the Pinecote Pavilion, designed by AIA Gold Medal winner E. Fay Jones, will soon undergo a significant restoration. Currently in the planning phase, the preservation of the Mississippi Landmark structure will be carried out in accordance with guidelines established by the Mississippi Department of Archives and History. Tom Howorth, principle architect and president of Howorth and Associates in Oxford, will lead the project. Funding has been allocated by the Mississippi Department of Finance and Administration through the Bureau of Building, and the project is anticipated to be bid in November 2013, and estimated for completion in June 2014.</p>

  <div class="two-column-layout">
    <div class="column">
      <div class="sub-heading">Senior Curator's Corner:</div>
      <p>Summer is upon us and the living is good! What a very busy spring we had. The weekend of April 5-7 was jam packed with good speakers and activities. If you haven't signed up to follow the ArboretumWordPress blog, you really should. For those who are not able to get to the Arboretum as much as they would like, there are post-event reports with images and sometimes links to video clips via our YouTube channel. I am happy to report we now have more than 500 followers on Twitter!</p>
      <p>The first 2013 Friday luncheon program with Dr. Wayne Porter was success. More than a dozen folks attended his wildflower lecture. I plan to continue them once a month, coordinating with our monthly Friday volunteer meetings.</p>
      <p>Speaking of Volunteers, Ms. Amy Lehn who joined the Volunteer program in March, has been a real firecracker out back in the greenhouse/nursery/garden sheds. Carrie and I have been working with her on a weekly Friday and Friday, Amy is developing tasks for Team Volunteers. If you want to join us, be sure to stop by the Arboretum or check out the website at <a href="http://www.crosbyarboretum.msstate.edu/pages/volunteerin.php">http://www.crosbyarboretum.msstate.edu/pages/volunteerin.php</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="column">
      <div class="image-container">
        <img src="placeholder_image_2.jpg" alt="Architectural detail">
        <p class="caption">Dr. Charles Allen leads walk at Hillsdale Bog, Sat. April 6, 2013. (Image by <span data-field="image_credit_2">{{ image_credit_2 }}</span>)</p>
      </div>
      <p>The south Gum Pond trail and the main path in the Swamp Forest Exhibit will begin construction soon, thanks to parent Annual Appeal funds donated by Crosby Arboretum members. We are also grateful for a community grant from Walnut that provided funding for two special events this spring: a March presentation on Wildlife Day by the Nature Institute's Wetlands Express, and April's well-attended Crosby Arboretum Lecture Series featuring Dr. Charles Allen on "Edible and Useful Plants of the Gulf South".</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="clear: both; margin-top: 20px;">MSU landscape architecture professor Bob Bruszek's spring landscape management seminar (LA 8721) met with Arboretum staff and conducted research to develop a comprehensive Pinecote Exhibit Management Manual. The document will guide the maintenance and development of the site's exhibits, and identifies best practices for management. Brazulec's co-taught a second class (ARC 4990) with MSU architecture professor Herman D. Smith, and presented their final concepts on April 6 to Crosby Arboretum Foundation Board members on the Pinecote Pavilion.</p>
</div>

